{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 560,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Imagine you are sitting at your kitchen table on a \nSunday morning. You are doing what I like to call  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 6680,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a financial deep clean. You look into your wallet \nand see that dusty old piece of plastic you got  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 13720,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "back when you were in college. It has a \nzero balance, a clunky rewards program  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 19080,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that barely earns you a cup of coffee once a \nyear, and you have not swiped it in 18 months.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 25480,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You think to yourself that closing this account \nis just good housekeeping. It is one less login  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 31120,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to remember, one less card to worry about losing, \nand it feels like a step toward a simpler life.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 38000,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You pick up the phone, call the bank, and \nsay those four words that feel so productive.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 43440,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I want to cancel. But here is the problem. While \nyou feel more organized, the algorithm at Fair  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 50280,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Isaac Corporation, the people who build your FICO \nscore, is already beginning to deduct points from  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 55920,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "your financial future. You did not just close \na card. You mathematically volunteered to pay  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 61880,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more for your next mortgage, your next car \nloan, and even your next insurance policy.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 68080,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Most people think they are just losing a bit \nof history. They think the damage is minor.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 73400,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They are wrong. It is a calculated error that \ncan haunt your credit report for a full decade.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 80880,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Before we dive into the specific math of \nwhy that college card is your most valuable  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 85680,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "financial asset, I want to make a deal with you. \nThe next 20 minutes might be the highest return on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 92720,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "investment you get all week. We are going to look \nat the gears and levers inside your credit score  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 99600,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that lenders never explain to you. If you \nfind this level of deep dive analysis useful,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 105640,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I would love for you to join our community \nhere. We call ourselves the optimizers.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 111640,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you click that join button, you get \npriority in the comments and some custom  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 115640,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "badges for the channel. It is how we \nkeep the lights on and the data coming.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 120720,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, to understand why closing that card is such \na disaster, we have to look at the FICO formula.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 127240,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is not a guessing game. It is a weighted \nsystem where 90% of top lenders use the same  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 132200,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "playbook. When you cancel that card, you are \nnot just hitting one category of your score.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 137840,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you are actually destabilizing \n45% of the total calculation.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 142800,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We are talking about two massive weights. Your \ncredit utilization and your length of history.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 148880,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let us break down how the math actually works \nwhen that account disappears. I have spent a lot  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 154680,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of time looking at the FICO model and it helps to \nthink of your credit score like a five layer cake.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 160880,
    "dDurationMs": 6680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The bottom layer is the biggest 35% for payment \nhistory. Basically, do you pay your bills?  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 167560,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Easy enough. But the next two layers are \nwhere the cancellation error happens.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 173120,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Amounts owed makes up 30%. And length \nof credit history makes up 15%.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 179600,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Together, those two categories control \nnearly half of your financial reputation.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 185280,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When people talk about their credit score, they \nusually focus on the 35%, the payment history.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 191480,
    "dDurationMs": 7720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But that is reactive. It only tracks what you have \nalready done. The 30% and the 15% are structural.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 199920,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They represent the current architecture of your \nfinancial life. When you close your oldest card,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 205640,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you are essentially taking a sledgehammer to \nthe foundation of those two structural layers.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 211280,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The FICO score 8 model, which is \nthe industry standard right now,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 214960,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is particularly sensitive to these shifts. It \nlooks for stability and available capacity.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 220920,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A closed account signals the opposite of \nstability to an algorithm. It looks like a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 225320,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reduction in your financial footprint. To the \ncomputer in a dark room at FICO headquarters,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 230920,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you did not just tidy up your wallet, you \neffectively shrank your creditworthiness. Let us  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 236720,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "look at why the first hit happens the very second \nthat card issuer sends a report to the bureaus.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 243280,
    "dDurationMs": 7560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let us talk about the 30% category, amounts owed. \nThis is a bit of a misnomer because the algorithm  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 250840,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cares less about the dollar amount and more \nabout the ratio. This is your credit utilization.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 258000,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It is a very simple division problem. Your \ntotal balances divided by your total limits.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 263600,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is where most people make their first \nmathematical error when cancelelling a card.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 268360,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They think if they have a zero balance on \nthat old card, closing it should not matter.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 273320,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But they are forgetting the denominator. Your \ntotal available credit is the sum of every limit  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 278440,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on every card you own. If you have three cards and \nyou close one, your total available credit drops.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 285360,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This might not seem like a big deal if you do \nnot carry much debt, but the math says otherwise.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 291120,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Consumer Financial Protection Bureau and \nmajor bureaus like Experian tell us to keep  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 295520,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this ratio below 30%. But if you want to score \nover 800, you really need that number below 10%.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 303120,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When you remove a limit, especially an old one \nthat has probably grown over the years through  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 307160,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "credit limit increases, you are making your \ndenominator smaller. This means any balance  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 312520,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you do carry on your other cards now represents \na much larger percentage of your total credit.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 318440,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You could wake up the next morning with a \ndoubled utilization rate without spending  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 322160,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an extra penny on a single latte. That \nis the immediate mathematical penalty.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 328440,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Wait until you see how this plays out in a real \nworld scenario because the numbers are startling.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 335400,
    "dDurationMs": 7160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let us look at a consumer I will call Alex. \nAlex has three cards. Card A is the oldest,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 342560,
    "dDurationMs": 8640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "10 years old with a $10,000 limit and a zero \nbalance. Card B is 5 years old with a $5,000 limit  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 351200,
    "dDurationMs": 8040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and a $2,000 balance. Card C is 2 years old \nwith another $5,000 limit and a $3,000 balance.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 360480,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Right now, Alex has $20,000 in total \ncredit and $5,000 in total debt.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 366840,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That is a 25% utilization rate. Alex is \ndoing fine. He is under that 30% threshold.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 374600,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But then Alex decides to tidy up. He \ncloses card A because he never uses it.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 381200,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Suddenly, his total available credit \nis not $20,000 anymore. It is $10,000.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 387560,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "His $5,000 debt did not go away, but now it \nis being measured against a much smaller pool.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 394160,
    "dDurationMs": 8280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Overnight, Alex went from 25% utilization to 50% \nutilization. In the eyes of the FICO algorithm,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 402440,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Alex just became twice as risky. He did not lose \nhis job. He did not miss a payment. And he did not  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 409080,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spend more money. He just performed a mathematical \nself-sabotage. For someone in Alex's position,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 416360,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this single move could trigger a 40point drop \nin his credit score within 30 days. That is the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 422320,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "difference between getting a prime interest rate \nand being bumped into a much more expensive tier.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 428360,
    "dDurationMs": 7840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, let us move to the 15% category, length of \ncredit history. This is where the most common myth  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 436200,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in personal finance lives. I hear people say all \nthe time that if you close your oldest card today,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 442800,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "your credit age drops to zero immediately. I \nam here to tell you that is actually a myth.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 448400,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the reality is much more dangerous because \nit is a ticking time bomb. Under the FICO model,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 454440,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which again is what most lenders actually use, \nan account closed in good standing does not just  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 459680,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "vanish. It stays on your report and continues to \nage for exactly 10 years from the date of closure.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 466720,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, if you close that 10-year-old card today, FICO \nstill counts it as a 10-year-old card tomorrow.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 473160,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You might be thinking, Tom, if it stays for 10 \nyears, why does everyone say it is a mistake?  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 479160,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The reason is twofold. First, you are \nlosing the anchor of your credit profile.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 484640,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Second, there is a massive difference between how \nFICO sees your history and how those free credit  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 489960,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "monitoring apps see it. If you are checking \nyour score on a site that uses Vantage Score,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 495200,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you will see a drop immediately because they \noften exclude closed accounts right away.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 500440,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But with FICO, the real pain is delayed. You \nare setting a trap for your future self that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 505800,
    "dDurationMs": 7640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "will spring in exactly one decade. If you think a \n40point drop from utilization is bad, wait until  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 513440,
    "dDurationMs": 7080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you see what happens at the 10-year mark. We call \nthis the 10-year cliff. Let us run those numbers.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 521040,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Imagine your current average age of accounts is 7 \nyears. That is considered a healthy, solid number.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 528120,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Your oldest card is 15 years old. If you close \nit today, it stays on your report for 10 years.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 534840,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Fast forward a decade. You have been \nresponsible. You have opened a couple of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 539360,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "new cards over the years, and your credit feels \ngreat. But on the anniversary of that closure,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 545000,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that old 15year-old account, which would have been \n25 years old by now, gets purged from your report.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 552080,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Suddenly, your average age of \naccounts does not just dip. It craters  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 557120,
    "dDurationMs": 3560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because you closed it 10 years ago. \nIt stopped being an active anchor.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 561400,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When it falls off, your average age might drop \nfrom 12 years down to 6 years in a single day.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 567480,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You have effectively robbed your future \nself of years of credit seasoning.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 571760,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It is like planting a tree and then cutting it \ndown right before it starts providing real shade.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 577360,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is why keeping that oldest line \nopen is about long-term strategy.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 582160,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It is the only way to ensure that as you open \nnew accounts in the future, your average age  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 587120,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stays high enough to absorb the impact. Without \nthat old anchor, every new card you get will drag  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 593240,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "your score down much further and much faster. \nThis leads us to what I call the anchor effect.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 600520,
    "dDurationMs": 3560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Think of your oldest credit card as \nthe lead weight on a fishing line.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 605240,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Every time you open a new account, whether it is \na car loan, a mortgage, or a new rewards card,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 611680,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you are adding a zero-year old account to your \naverage. If your oldest card is only 3 years old,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 618880,
    "dDurationMs": 3240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a new account is going to pull \nyour average down significantly.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 623560,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But if your oldest card is 20 years old, \nthat heavy weight keeps the average from  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 628360,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "moving too much. It stabilizes your score \nagainst the volatility of new credit.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 635120,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Most people stop their analysis at the immediate \nscore drop, but they miss the opportunity cost.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 641520,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "By closing that old card, you are making it \nharder for yourself to take advantage of new  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 646120,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "financial opportunities later. Want to open a \nnew travel card to get a massive sign up bonus?  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 653240,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you closed your old Anchor card, that \nnew application is going to hit your score  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 657720,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "twice as hard. You have essentially removed \nthe shock absorbers from your credit report.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 664320,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is where the math of the 15% history category \nmeets the math of the 10% new credit category.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 671480,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You are making your entire profile more \nfragile. In the language of risk analysis,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 677120,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "closing that card is an action with 0% \nupside and 100% downside for your score.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 684080,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But as I always say, personal finance \nis not played out on a calculator alone.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 690160,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is the part where conventional wisdom \nsays you should never ever close the card.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 696600,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But I am going to tell you that \nsometimes you absolutely should.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 701360,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Financial literacy requires nuance and there \nare three specific scenarios where closing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 706640,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "your oldest card is actually the smart \nmove, even if the math says otherwise.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 713400,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The first is predatory annual fees. Many \npeople start their credit journey with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 718480,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "what we call subprime lenders. Companies that \ntarget people with no credit or bad credit.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 725200,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These cards often come with monthly maintenance \nfees or high annual fees that offer zero rewards.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 732000,
    "dDurationMs": 8400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If your oldest card is charging you $10 a month \njust to exist, that is $120 a year. Over a decade,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 740400,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you are paying over $1,000 just to prop up a \ncredit score. No five or 10point boost is worth  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 747400,
    "dDurationMs": 7240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "$1,000 of unreoverable cash. In that case, the fee \nis a leak in your boat that needs to be plugged.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 756280,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The second scenario is psychological. If having \nthat open credit line represents a temptation  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 762000,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that leads you into highinterest debt, the \nmath of your credit score no longer matters.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 767920,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Paying 24% interest on a balance is a much \nbigger financial disaster than a temporary  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 772880,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dip in your FICO score. Your mental health and \nyour ability to stay out of debt are worth more  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 779160,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than any algorithm's opinion of you. Finally, \nthere is the legal necessity of joint accounts.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 786680,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If that old card is tied to an ex-spouse, you need \nto sever that tie immediately. The risk of them  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 793000,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "maxing out the card and ruining your credit is far \nhigher than the risk of you closing it yourself.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 799480,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But what if you could have the best of both \nworlds? What if you could stop paying that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 803800,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "annual fee without killing your oldest credit \nline? There is a strategy that the big banks  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 810080,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "do not exactly advertise, but it is one of the \nmost powerful tools in your financial toolkit.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 816120,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It is called a product change. Instead of \ncanceling the card, you call the issuer,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 821720,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Chase, MX, City, whoever it is, and you ask to \ndowngrade the account to a no annual fee version.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 829360,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is the mathematical equivalent of a \ncheat code. When you do a product change,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 834320,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the bank typically keeps your existing credit \nlimit, which protects your 30% utilization score.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 840600,
    "dDurationMs": 3800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But even better, they keep the same \ntrade line open on your credit report.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 845320,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That means your 10-year-old account stays a \n10-year-old account. It just has a different name  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 850280,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and a different piece of plastic in your wallet. \nYou have effectively stopped the bleeding of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 855040,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "annual fee while keeping your credit history and \nyour utilization denominator perfectly intact.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 861520,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I've done this myself with cards I no longer \nfound useful. It takes 5 minutes on the phone  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 867200,
    "dDurationMs": 3320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and it saves your score from that \n10-year cliff we talked about earlier.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 871600,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is how the optimizers handle their credit. We \ndo not just accept the choices the bank gives us.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 877160,
    "dDurationMs": 3880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We look for the path that keeps our math \nworking for us instead of against us.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 882160,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Once you have successfully downgraded that card \nor decided to keep it, you cannot just cut it up  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 887920,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and forget about it. This is a common mistake that \nleads to what I call the involuntary cancellation.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 895200,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Credit card issuers are businesses and they do \nnot like keeping inactive accounts on their books.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 901280,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you do not use a card for 12 to 24 months, \nthe bank will often close it for you due to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 906400,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "inactivity. If they close it, you suffer all the \nmathematical penalties we have been discussing,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 912520,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but you lose the control over when it happens. \nTo prevent this, you need the sock draw protocol.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 919360,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You take that old card and you tie one small \nrecurring monthly charge to it. I am talking about  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 925280,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "something tiny, a $3 iCloud storage fee, a Spotify \nsubscription, or even a small recurring donation.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 933360,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Then, and this is the vital part, you set that \ncard to autopay the full statement balance  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 938760,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "every single month from your checking account. \nOnce that is done, the card is technically active  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 944440,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the eyes of the bank. They see activity, they \nsee on-time payments, and they keep the line open.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 950960,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You can literally put the physical card in a sock \ndrawer or even destroy it if you are worried about  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 955680,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fraud. The digital record, the trade line, stays \nalive. This ensures that your oldest account  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 962240,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "continues to age, your utilization denominator \nstays high, and you never pay a scent in interest.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 969000,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It is a set it and forget it strategy for \nan elite credit score. Before we wrap up,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 975040,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we need to address why you might see \nconflicting information about this online.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 980440,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There is a reason your friend might tell you their \nscore didn't drop at all when they closed a card.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 985680,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It comes down to the battle between FICO and \nVantage Score. Most of the apps you use for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 991400,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "free credit monitoring use Vantage Score \n3.0. This model is much more aggressive.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 999240,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you close a card, Vantage Score often stops \ncounting it in your age calculation immediately.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1006120,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is why people check their app a week after \nclosing a card and see a massive drop. They panic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1012680,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and assume their FICO score did the same thing. \nBut remember, FICO is the one that lenders  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1018800,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "actually use for mortgages and car loans. FICO is \nthe one that gives you that 10-year grace period.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1026160,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It is important to know which scoreboard \nyou are looking at. If you are preparing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1030600,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to buy a home in the next 2 years, do not make \nany moves based on what a free app tells you.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1037200,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Look at the FICO math. Understanding \nthe difference between these two models  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1042600,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is what separates casual observers from \npeople who actually master their finances.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1048400,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You have to know which rules the person on \nthe other side of the desk is playing by.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1053400,
    "dDurationMs": 7400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And in the world of high stakes lending, FICO \nis still the king. So what is the final verdict?  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1061560,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Closing your oldest credit card is in almost \nevery case a profound mathematical error.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1067720,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You are volunteering to shrink your credit \ncapacity which spikes your utilization.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1072680,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You are setting a countdown for your average \nage of accounts to crater a decade from now.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1077680,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And you are removing the anchor that protects \nyour score from the impact of new credit.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1083440,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Unless you are being charged an \nexorbitant fee that you cannot negotiate  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1087520,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or you are protecting yourself from \nthe psychological trap of overspending,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1091560,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that card is worth more to you open than closed. \nCredit scoring is not an emotional process.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1099080,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The algorithm does not care that you want a clean \nwallet. It only cares about the data points.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1105160,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "By keeping that old account active with a tiny \nrecurring charge and autopay, you are making  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1110280,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the algorithm work for you. You are building \na history that will eventually span decades,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1115920,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "making you look like the most stable, reliable \nborrower in the world. That stability translates  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1121800,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into lower interest rates, which translates \ninto thousands of dollars kept in your pocket  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1126440,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "over your lifetime. It is a simple game of \narithmetic, and now you know how to win it.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1133240,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It is about playing the long game in a world that \nis obsessed with the short term. Your future self  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1138920,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "will thank you for the Sunday morning you \ndecided not to cancel that dusty old card.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1144360,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I want to hear from you now. Have you ever closed \nan old account and watched your score react?  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1150720,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or maybe you have successfully pulled off a \nproduct change with a bank that tried to tell you  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1154960,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it was not possible. Drop a comment below. I treat \nthese comments as a form of peer review and data  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1162000,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gathering for our community. We learn more when \nwe share our actual experiences with these banks.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1168960,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you found this breakdown helpful, consider \nhitting that join button to become an optimizer.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1174680,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It is the best way to support the deep \ndive research we do here, and it helps  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1178840,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "me keep these videos free of sponsored fluff \nthat doesn't actually help your bottom line.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1184680,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I have put a lot of work into the sources for this \nvideo, and everything we discussed is backed by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1189600,
    "dDurationMs": 7560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the latest FICO and regulatory data. Managing your \nmoney is a series of small calculated decisions  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1197160,
    "dDurationMs": 2640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and you just made a great \none by finishing this video.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1200920,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I am Tom and this is Tom Talks Money. I will \nsee you in the next one where we are going to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1206600,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "look at another piece of conventional wisdom \nthat might be costing you more than you think.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1211960,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Keep those anchors heavy and those \nutilization rates light. I will see you then."
    } ]
  } ]
}
